{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The target redefines 'constrain' as a pure property of the static import graph — that a test constrains a subject if and only if the test's import graph reaches the mutated code path — but this is self-sealing and empirically non-responsive. If any surviving mutation can be pointed to in a module whose import graph is saturated, the target claims this confirms topology; if a mutation is lost despite an imported subject, the target retreats to skip behavior or installation order, making the claim unfalsifiable as stated. It asserts that 'the 1/9 mutation was in a module with no test importer' without evidence, and it attributes the 4/4 to 6/7 survivals in ordinarily-guarded modules to import saturation without showing that their mutants actually lie on reached code paths. The offered topology mechanism therefore does not explain the observed distribution; it restates it in graph language.","premise":"The problem asks for a condition under which a test constrains rather than describes, and for an account of the 3-of-26 result and its distribution; the target substitutes an unevidenced import-graph explanation and an 'iff' criterion that can absorb any outcome.","premise_evidence":null,"counterexample":["Suppose the ordinary modules include surviving mutants on fully reached and executed paths (import graph saturated), while compile.py's single lost mutant is also in an imported and executed module but was skipped only under shown-to-fail-first installation. On the target's 'iff' topology criterion both should constrain, yet one mutant survives and the other is lost. The target must then invoke skips or installation order, conceding that import reach is not the operative constraint condition.","Suppose every module has a saturated import graph. The target's stated account of the asymmetry collapses, because topology is uniform while 3 of 26 mutations still survive; the criterion predicts no difference and so cannot account for the result it was offered to explain."]}]}